From af25cd38223f9dbf0b1d10ca47c029a60bd142d2 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sat, 25 Jul 2026 20:33:54 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Add non-negativity and Tikhonov regularization to the fODF
 deconvolution

The fODF deconvolution was an unregularized least squares fit, which is
ill-conditioned because the kernel rotational invariants decay quickly
with l, so the estimated fODFs have large negative lobes at clinical SNR.

Two optional regularizers are added to get_plm_from_S_and_kernel:

- Non-negativity, following constrained spherical deconvolution
  (Tournier et al., NeuroImage 2007): the fODF is initialized with a low
  order unconstrained fit and refined iteratively, penalizing its
  amplitude on the directions where it falls below tau*mean(fODF) until
  that set of directions stops changing.
- Tikhonov damping lambda^2*||Gamma*plm||^2, with Gamma either the
  identity or the Laplace-Beltrami matrix diag(l(l+1)).

Both weights are dimensionless, the regularization blocks are rescaled
by the norm of the rows of the design matrix of each voxel. Both are
disabled by default, so the deconvolution is unchanged unless
options.fODF_regularization is used.

New static methods: fODF_RegularizationDefaults (option parsing and
Tikhonov matrix), plm_regularized_deconvolution (per voxel solver), and
GetUniformHemisphereDirs (directions where non-negativity is imposed).
The options used, the number of iterations, the number of constrained
directions, and a convergence flag per voxel are returned in
out.fODF_regularization and written to the log file.

Also adds example_fODF_regularization.m, a synthetic two-fiber example
that compares the deconvolutions and verifies that the noise free
deconvolution recovers the ground truth plm exactly.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01ELDbwN8nCdUQVoKkVv7yhH
